It's great that you're interested in the teachings and values of the Boy Scouts. The Boy Scouts of America (BSA) has a strong emphasis on character development, citizenship, and personal fitness. They encourage young people to do their best in every situation and to serve others. The BSA has a code of conduct that guides its members in their daily lives and in their interactions with others.

One of the key principles of the BSA is the Scout Oath, which members recite during meetings and activities. The oath includes the following elements:

1. A solemn promise to do my duty to God and my country, and to obey the Scout Law;
2. To help other people at all times;
3. To keep myself physically strong, mentally awake, and morally straight;
4. To build my own character and develop my skills in service to others.

Members of the BSA are also encouraged to follow the 12 points of the Scout Law, which include being trustworthy, loyal, and helping others.

The BSA also offers various ranks and merit badges that scouts can work towards, which involve learning new skills and demonstrating their mastery of those skills. This helps scouts to develop their confidence and abilities.

